{
  "@context": {
    "schema": "https://schema.org/"
  },
  "@type": "Note",
  "@id": "note:notion-217",
  "title": "Prologue",
  "status": "reviewing",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "b-1",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Prologue"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-2",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "I am often asked why I, a Black American woman who was born of racial enslavement and Jim Crow on US shores, decided to write about antiblackness in France"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-3",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "rather than “in your own country.” The short answer involves a long and ambivalent"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-4",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "relationship with la Métropole—over more years than I care to divulge—where I"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-5",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "have experienced up close its “Black Paris” of expatriate lore, its “Afrique/Antillessur-Seine,” and its wretched underside where economic precarity and abusive policing inflict immeasurable damage. Then there is my dear mother’s wisdom and"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-6",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "example that have continually taught me that we must fight “the battle” wherever"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-7",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "we are; and so it has been. The longer answer lies in the pages ahead, in the lived experiences of French people racialized-as-black in an officially raceblind France that"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-8",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "negates both their existence and the antiblackness suffered in the everyday. Much,"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-9",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "then, of what I have witnessed and been subjected to has inspired this book, which"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-10",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "is ultimately about violence, making it extremely painful to research and equally"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-11",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "painful to write. With that, I am also mindful of vicariously reproducing what I"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-12",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "critique in this book—so much so that it often felt like a difficult and delicate balancing act between illustrating very real danger and injuries endured on the ground"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-13",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "and adding to that violence in turn. At the same time, I strongly feel that to exclude"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-14",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "or “whitewash” my findings or the daily realities that many experience would be"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-15",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "to err on the side of the raceblind state. The French state’s policy and practices of"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-16",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "dismissing and/or camouflaging antiblackness in society powerfully contributes to"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-17",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "its perpetuation. Antiblackness is a hydra whose venom is not always most potent"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-18",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "in visible or explicit actions. Instead, it often manifests itself in the subtlest of ways"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-19",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "due to having been racialized-as-black and treated as less-than on some level in"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-20",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "French society. This was memorably illustrated to me and a long-term mentor and"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-21",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "friend on a late summer evening in 2014—though it could have happened yesterday,"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-22",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "and similar violations are happening as I write."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-23",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "It was Paris as usual in the Latin Quarter where I lived at the time. The avenues"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-24",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "and cafés were teeming with the hustle and bustle of daily life. Street musicians,"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-25",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Keaton, Trica. #You Know You're Black in France When : The Fact of Everyday Antiblackness, MIT Press, 2023. ProQuest Ebook Central,"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-26",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "[http://ebookcentral.proquest.com/lib/dartmouth-ebooks/detail.action?docID=29673053](http://ebookcentral.proquest.com/lib/dartmouth-ebooks/detail.action?docID=29673053)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-27",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Created from dartmouth-ebooks on 2023-03-24 17:19:30. Copyright © 2023. MIT Press. All rights reserved."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-28",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "x Prologue"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-29",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Capoeira artists, and rose peddlers vied for attention. Errant tourists and Parisian"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-30",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "habitué dashed about the boulevard in search of adventure, libations, or victuals,"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-31",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "while Moussa and I soaked it all in over dinner to the serenade of car horns and the"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-32",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "occasional cathedral bells tolling in the distance.1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-33",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "As often happens, our conversation turned to the topic of this book, and, because Moussa is also a provocateur, it"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-34",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "didn’t surprise me when he said at one point, “Trica, you know, things are getting"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-35",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "better in France.” His wry smile, however, suggested otherwise. As I began to cite"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-36",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "chapter and verse from my research, our conversation was cut short. Already, it"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-37",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "was past midnight, and we were the only two people left in the bistro, save for the"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-38",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "waiters who had started their closing ritual of power-stacking chairs and tables that"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-39",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "inched ever-closer to where we were seated. On the opposite side of our table stood"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-40",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "the unsolicited host with a credit card machine ready in hand. He had that “please"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-41",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "go already” look on his face, a not-so-subtle way of saying that we could stay, as the"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-42",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "expression goes, but not there. At his recommendation, we decided to continue"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-43",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "what had become a debate across the street at the Antoine—or at least that was the"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-44",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "plan. The Antoine was perfect because it is one of the few bistros that stays open"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-45",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "well past the witching hour, making it a magnet for the after-hours crowd who is"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-46",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "unready or unwilling to let Paris sleep."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-47",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Cosmetically, Moussa may be right. B/black people2"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-48",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "in urban metropolitan"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-49",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "centers like Paris seem visible everywhere, including in everyday occupations"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-50",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "(salespeople, waiters, bank tellers, post office clerks, and so on) that involve direct"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-51",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "interaction with the public. A generation ago in the Metropole, people of color were"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-52",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "conspicuously absent even in these common jobs, and/or were simply hidden from"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-53",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "public view, while overrepresented in kitchens, in backrooms as civil servants, or"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-54",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "in offices as after-hours custodial staff. In positions of power and decision-making,"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-55",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "from higher education to government to cinema and television to journalism to"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-56",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "captains of industry, on en est loin. There’s still much unfinished work. I confess"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-57",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "my own astonishment in 2014 when I saw the first b/Black teller in my bank who,"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-58",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "by 2016, had been promoted to a front-office manager in the 6th arrondissement."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-59",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "When I shared my surprise with him, he simply said, “Things have evolved.” Still,"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-60",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "that the presence of b/Black and non-whitened people is remarkable in public, or"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-61",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "their absence unremarkable, only discloses whiteness as the norm, making everything else the exception. This “absent-present” minimal self, as Stuart Hall writes,"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-62",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "“is and isn’t there,” for the blackened body is seen but is racially overdetermined,"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-63",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "hence also unseen as a human being whose recognition, as philosopher Katherine"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-64",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "McKittrick writes, “can expose domination as a visible spatial project that organizes,"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-65",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Keaton, Trica. #You Know You're Black in France When : The Fact of Everyday Antiblackness, MIT Press, 2023. ProQuest Ebook Central,"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-66",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "[http://ebookcentral.proquest.com/lib/dartmouth-ebooks/detail.action?docID=29673053](http://ebookcentral.proquest.com/lib/dartmouth-ebooks/detail.action?docID=29673053)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-67",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Created from dartmouth-ebooks on 2023-03-24 17:19:30. Copyright © 2023. MIT Press. All rights reserved."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-68",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Prologue xi"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-69",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "names, and sees social differences . . . and determines where social order happens.”3"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-70",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Later that evening at the Antoine, when the police were called, this fact of"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-71",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "antiblackness would be borne out, illustrating McKittrick’s pointed observations"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-72",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "that “[anti-b]lack matters are [also] spatial matters” in everyday France.4"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-73",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Comfortably seated on the terrace, second row center, facing the boulevard with"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-74",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "nothing obstructing our view, I noticed that Moussa and I were the only people"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-75",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "visibly of color in the bistro. But then, a man and woman arrived, a couple with"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-76",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "whom I had earlier exchanged a head nod of mutual recognition when crossing"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-77",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "the street with Moussa on our way to the Antoine. Rather than choosing one of the"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-78",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "other prime spots on the terrace, as we had, they opted instead for a back table on"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-79",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "its fringes, an area tailor-made for intimacy or a bit of quiet away from the throngs."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-80",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Later, they would share that they were married, on holiday from Belgium, and, to"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-81",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "complicate identity politics, the husband said that he was also Moroccan. However,"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-82",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "his dark skin tone, features, and hair texture, like those of his wife, signified blackness as opposed to the customary scripting of someone from Morocco as “Arab,”"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-83",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "even as these identities are not mutually exclusive, and b/Black people are indigenous to that region."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-84",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The ambiance in the bistro reminded me of what a musician friend calls “cool"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-85",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "jazz.” Unobtrusive melodies played in the background, sweetening the whispers of"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-86",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "neighboring voices that fell gently on the ear while alcoholic elixirs of every sort"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-87",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "yielded their fruit within the embrace of a temperate August night. As we took it all"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-88",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "in, a line from writer Bernard Dadié’s satirical novel, An African in Paris (Un nègre à"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-89",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Paris), captured well that sublime moment, indeed the stuff on which the City of"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-90",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Light’s reputation is built: “Yes, my friend, Paris is all that—and more. And no one,"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-91",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "absolutely no one, can resist her call.” But just as that feeling began to cradle us, it"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-92",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "was utterly shattered by the arrival of four loud, white-appearing individuals, three"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-93",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "men and one woman, perhaps in their late twenties, who plopped down nearly in"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-94",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "front of Moussa and me as if they owned the joint. Their pitched voices, slurred"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-95",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "speech, florid faces, red-rimmed eyes, and general demeanor were telltale signs"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-96",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "that the evening, for them, was far from young. Impatient to be served, the selfappointed leader headed to the bar, shouting “Beer for everyone,” just as the waiter"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-97",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "was approaching their table. Once served, they regaled each other with a story about"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-98",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "some hapless person from an earlier leg of their evening, which inspired “Monsieur"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-99",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Beer for Everyone” to bolt to the middle of the road in front of the Antoine, where"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-100",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Keaton, Trica. #You Know You're Black in France When : The Fact of Everyday Antiblackness, MIT Press, 2023. ProQuest Ebook Central,"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-101",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "[http://ebookcentral.proquest.com/lib/dartmouth-ebooks/detail.action?docID=29673053](http://ebookcentral.proquest.com/lib/dartmouth-ebooks/detail.action?docID=29673053)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-102",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Created from dartmouth-ebooks on 2023-03-24 17:19:30. Copyright © 2023. MIT Press. All rights reserved."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-103",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "xii Prologue"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-104",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "he performed an elaborate Charlie Chaplinesque impersonation, presumably of"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-105",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "that hapless person."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-106",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "For the grand finale, aided no doubt by liquid courage, “Monsieur Beer” then"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-107",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "climbed onto a nearby bike, chained to a streetlamp, and, while steadying himself against the post with one hand and the other arm extended, he attempted a"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-108",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "pirouette en pointe that sent his friends into hysterics but him nearly crashing to"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-109",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "the ground. Baby-step style and on wobbly legs, he dismounted from the bike and"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-110",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "bowed to shouts of “bravo” and applause that rang out from his friends. However,"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-111",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "my thoughts drifted to the unfortunate owner of the bike. I pictured this person"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-112",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "sitting unawares on the seat where an inebriated and therefore less attentive “Monsieur Beer” had been standing in shoes that had traversed dog-friendly Parisian"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-113",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "sidewalks that are all too frequently streaked by what the French call les pipis sauvages (“wild peeing”), or as a friend described it, “males decorating walls and sidewalks,” which flows from the corners of buildings used for quick relief. On many a"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-114",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "night, I have leaped over this “scourge of urban life, costing millions of dollars for"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-115",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "cleaning and the repair of damage to public infrastructure. And, oh, the stench.”5"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-116",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "By then, the cool jazz ambiance was obliterated, along with any hope of resuming our discussion, so Moussa and I decided to call it a night. But our waiter was"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-117",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "already occupied with Monsieur Beer and company who refused to pay their bill,"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-118",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "causing the waiter to call in the manager, and from there, things escalated. Slurring his words and apparently speaking for the group, Monsieur Beer attempted"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-119",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "to express his displeasure with the quality of the beer that they had nevertheless"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-120",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "mostly consumed. Still refusing to settle, the manager then threatened to call the"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-121",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "police but was waved off with a dismissive flick of the hand. Then, both the waiter"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-122",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "and manager stormed off, making Moussa and me an unwilling captive audience"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-123",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "because we couldn’t leave until we’d paid our bill, and there was no one to take"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-124",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "our money."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-125",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Shortly after, an unmarked dark car pulled up in front of the Antoine. Three"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-126",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "doors opened quickly. Three white-appearing men in plainclothes exited who, without missing a beat, made a beeline for the Belgium couple. It was unclear at first"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-127",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "who the white men were, but all was clarified once the couple pulled out their wallets and presented these men with their “papers.” In other words, it was an identity"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-128",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "control of people “guilty of blackness,” as philosopher Lewis Gordon writes, meaning people for whom racialized blackness is the evidence of their supposed offense.6"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-129",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "While these law enforcers were inspecting the couple’s identification, the waiter"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-130",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "rushed to their table, quickly apologized, and escorted them to the actual offenders"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-131",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Keaton, Trica. #You Know You're Black in France When : The Fact of Everyday Antiblackness, MIT Press, 2023. ProQuest Ebook Central,"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-132",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "[http://ebookcentral.proquest.com/lib/dartmouth-ebooks/detail.action?docID=29673053](http://ebookcentral.proquest.com/lib/dartmouth-ebooks/detail.action?docID=29673053)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-133",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Created from dartmouth-ebooks on 2023-03-24 17:19:30. Copyright © 2023. MIT Press. All rights reserved."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-134",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Prologue xiii"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-135",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "whose nonchalance betrayed no signs of fear at the arrival of police. Instead, they"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-136",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "were arrogant and confident. At that moment, Moussa and I turned toward each"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-137",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "other with that “Did you just see what I just saw” look. Then my jaw tightened. Next"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-138",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "came that familiar bitter taste, accompanied by a dull pang in the gut where no"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-139",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "physical contact is required. The teeth grind. The heart thumps rapidly and audibly"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-140",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "in the ear, accompanied by that familiar flavor of blood in the mouth from biting"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-141",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "reflexively the tongue or lip, while that age-old feeling of disgust sinks in from the"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-142",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "violence of the familiar, from the lived experience of everyday antiblackness."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-143",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In seeing only blackened bodies, the law enforcers became perpetrators of a"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-144",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "routine violation of another order, one all too common in French and other European societies where race is denied on the grounds of official raceblindness. This"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-145",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "fact of antiblackness is sometimes so fugitive and/or implicit that it becomes imperceptible even, at times, to those on the receiving end. Yet, in raceblind contexts, antiblackness renders visible the body racialized-as-black while raceblindness renders"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-146",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "invisible the role of race in the assault. Blackened bodies, simultaneously absent"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-147",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "and present, necessitate justification in spaces overdetermined by whiteness where"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-148",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "they are rendered antithetical “space invaders” vis-à-vis that which has “the ability"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-149",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "to pass as the universal human,” writes sociologist Nirmal Puwar.7"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-150",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Indeed, Frantz"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-151",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Fanon vividly captures the everydayness of this lived experience that reverberates"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-152",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "throughout a racialized world:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-153",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "I am overdetermined from without. I am a slave not to the “idea” that others have of me, but"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-154",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "of my own appearance. . . . Already the white gaze, the only legitimate one, is dissecting me. I"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-155",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "am fixed. Having sharpened their microtome, they objectively cut my reality into pieces. I am"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-156",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "betrayed. I feel, I see in those white eyes that it is not a new man entering, but a new type of"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-157",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "man. A new species. Why it’s a nègre!"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-158",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "8"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-159",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Now facing the actual culprits, the officers asked each of them in quiet tones for"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-160",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "their identification, while exchanging a bit of light banter and laughs during this"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-161",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "surreally real scene. Empowered by their presence, the manager demanded more"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-162",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "forcefully payment for the beer and even drank a gulp of it to attest to its quality."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-163",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Once more, this group simply shrugged it off. Even as they were told to pay their"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-164",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "bill, it was a very light moment of exchanging jokes and smiles. No humiliation, no"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-165",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "violence, only conviviality, which stands in stark contrast to what I have known and"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-166",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "researched over the years in mainland France. I looked at Moussa who seemed to"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-167",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "understand that I was about to raise my voice, but he gestured quietly but firmly"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-168",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Keaton, Trica. #You Know You're Black in France When : The Fact of Everyday Antiblackness, MIT Press, 2023. ProQuest Ebook Central,"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-169",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "[http://ebookcentral.proquest.com/lib/dartmouth-ebooks/detail.action?docID=29673053](http://ebookcentral.proquest.com/lib/dartmouth-ebooks/detail.action?docID=29673053)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-170",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Created from dartmouth-ebooks on 2023-03-24 17:19:30. Copyright © 2023. MIT Press. All rights reserved."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-171",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "xiv Prologue"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-172",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "with his head “no,” realizing that I could be charged with insulting an officer (outrage)—a serious charge in France—had I said what was on my mind. With a parting"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-173",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "goodbye, the officers departed as quickly as they arrived, not once glancing in the"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-174",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "direction of the couple nor offering an apology to people who had been “overdetermined from without.”"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-175",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Shortly thereafter, the group of four also left, while the angry manager looked"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-176",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "on with contempt. As they hailed a taxi on the other side of the street, we could"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-177",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "once more hear their drunken laughter, which seemed to linger in the air as they"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-178",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "drove off into the night. At that moment, the manager was not the only one angry,"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-179",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "but our reasons differed."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-180",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "When Moussa and I later spoke with the couple from Belgium, we learned that"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-181",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "they were neither shaken nor shocked; it was not the first time they had been unjustly approached by law enforcers in both France and Belgium, nor would it likely"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-182",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "be the last. In countries officially blind to race and/as color, the frequency, magnitude, and pattern of such policing remain difficult to measure statistically and go"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-183",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "under-reported, leaving a constellation of disconnected and unmapped violations"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-184",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "both far and wide. Here, I’m reminded of music scholar William Cheng’s prophetic"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-185",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "insights that speak to the pitfalls of raceblindness. Replace “we/us” with France."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-186",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "“We like to think that looking past [racialized] identity means we’ve done right by"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-187",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "other people,” writes Cheng, as if “such imaginative fictions let us off the hook.”9"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-188",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Before parting ways, the four of us entertained the proverbial “What if” question"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-189",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "that always seems to accompany antiblack attacks of this nature: What if the roles"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-190",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "had been reversed? What if Monsieur Beer and friends had been b/Black or some"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-191",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "other racially dehumanized people? The experiences of disparate treatment that I"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-192",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "document offer little optimism for a similar outcome in a French Republic currently"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-193",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "far, very far, from being blind to race or being “off the hook.”"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-194",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "However, little did I know on that fateful night that, five months later, Paris;"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-195",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Baga, Nigeria; and my personal life would forever change. The horrifying terrorist"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-196",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "massacres in 2015 and 2016 in some ways achieved their end by amplifying hatred,"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-197",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "fear, and insecurity amid states of perpetual war seemingly throughout the world."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-198",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The state of emergency that followed in France, lasting nearly two years, ushered in"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-199",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "counterterrorism policing that became a device to justify aggressive tactics, targeting people racialized-as-arab and black who were also victims of that terror. These"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-200",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "measures begged the question of whether sacrificing civil liberties was worth the"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-201",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "price of a ticket that seemed to offer dubious security gains and perhaps even worsened them. With these tragedies came another: the European refugee crisis that"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-202",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Keaton, Trica. #You Know You're Black in France When : The Fact of Everyday Antiblackness, MIT Press, 2023. ProQuest Ebook Central,"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-203",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "[http://ebookcentral.proquest.com/lib/dartmouth-ebooks/detail.action?docID=29673053](http://ebookcentral.proquest.com/lib/dartmouth-ebooks/detail.action?docID=29673053)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-204",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Created from dartmouth-ebooks on 2023-03-24 17:19:30. Copyright © 2023. MIT Press. All rights reserved."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-205",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Prologue xv"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-206",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "hit with a ferocity the French port city of Calais from January 2015 to October 2016."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-207",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Situated opposite the English Channel, Calais became the waystation for thousands"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-208",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "of migrants, largely from countries in Africa and the Middle East who were desperately fleeing war and/or poverty with the hope of finding relief in the United"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-209",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Kingdom. Instead, families, lone children, and individuals became stranded in a"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-210",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "squalid encampment that swelled to over six thousand people. This “Calais jungle,”"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-211",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "as it was tellingly dubbed by the media, was described by Human Rights Watch as"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-212",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "“hell.” Still, more convenient enemies could not have been better scripted for antiimmigrant groups and political campaigns that promised a return to supposedly"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-213",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "more bucolic, harmonious, and fantastical white-only times, and, for some, 1930s"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-214",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Europe. Parts of the encampment were set ablaze, some say “in retaliation,” following the November 2015 Paris terror-murders that claimed 130 lives and left hundreds"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-215",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "physically injured with unimaginable emotional costs in a France still reeling from"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-216",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "the January 2015 Charlie Hebdo attack. By October 2016, authorities evacuated the"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-217",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "camp entirely without addressing the cause of their migration. In 2017, a reported"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-218",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "thousand more returned, but instead of receiving humanitarian relief, they were"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-219",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "met with shock policing, supposedly aimed at preventing another encampment."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-220",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Human Rights Watch and other humanitarian groups further reported"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-221",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "police abuse of asylum seekers and migrants, their disruption of humanitarian assistance,"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-222",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "and their harassment of aid workers . . . by the use of violent force . . . police in Calais, particularly the riot police (Compagnies républicaines de sécurité, CRS), routinely use pepper"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-223",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "spray on child and adult migrants while they are sleeping or in other circumstances in which"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-224",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "they pose no threat; regularly spray or confiscate sleeping bags, blankets, and clothing; and"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-225",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "sometimes use pepper spray on migrants’ food and water. Police also disrupt the delivery of"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-226",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "humanitarian assistance. Police abuses have a negative impact on access to child services"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-227",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "and migrants’ desire and ability to apply for asylum.10"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-228",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Impacting, indeed eliminating, that desire is precisely the point, as seen along the"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-229",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "US southern border in detention centers where children have been separated from"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-230",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "their parents and caged in inhumane, deadly conditions. And who could forget"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-231",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "the grim images of Haitian asylum seekers on the US/Mexico borders in 2021? The"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-232",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "brutality and whipping of b/Black human beings by horse-mounted border police"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-233",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "not only echo slave patrols but also demonstrated how the past is prologue. Along"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-234",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "the southern borders of some European countries, lifeless bodies and scattered"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-235",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "possessions wash ashore, as Manthia Diawara depicts in his poignant film, Opera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-236",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Keaton, Trica. #You Know You're Black in France When : The Fact of Everyday Antiblackness, MIT Press, 2023. ProQuest Ebook Central,"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-237",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "[http://ebookcentral.proquest.com/lib/dartmouth-ebooks/detail.action?docID=29673053](http://ebookcentral.proquest.com/lib/dartmouth-ebooks/detail.action?docID=29673053)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-238",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Created from dartmouth-ebooks on 2023-03-24 17:19:30. Copyright © 2023. MIT Press. All rights reserved."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-239",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "xvi Prologue"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-240",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "of the World."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-241",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "11 And it continues. A desensitized public sees mere debris to be hauled"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-242",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "away without ever asking who and what set people flowing, to borrow from Farah"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-243",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Griffin’s classic work on African American dislocation and migration."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-244",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Then, on February 17, 2015, I lost the most important person in my life—my"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-245",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "mom—who abruptly and unexpectedly died, an amazing woman who survived the"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-246",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "worst that Jane and Jim Crow USA meted out and often thrived against those very"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-247",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "conditions. When thinking of my mom and my struggles to breathe life into this"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-248",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "book, I am reminded of immortal words attributed to bell hooks: “Sometimes people try to destroy you, precisely because they recognize your power—not because"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-249",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "they don’t see it, but because they see it and they don’t want it to exist.”12 I continue"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-250",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "to draw from my mom’s example to share with you through these pages the evidence of antiblackness mis-seen."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-251",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Keaton, Trica. #You Know You're Black in France When : The Fact of Everyday Antiblackness, MIT Press, 2023. ProQuest Ebook Central,"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-252",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "[http://ebookcentral.proquest.com/lib/dartmouth-ebooks/detail.action?docID=29673053](http://ebookcentral.proquest.com/lib/dartmouth-ebooks/detail.action?docID=29673053)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-253",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Created from dartmouth-ebooks on 2023-03-24 17:19:30. Copyright © 2023. MIT Press. All rights reserved."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}